#!/bin/sh
# Managed by pyinfra. Source: dotfiles/deadman/files/mail-deadman
#
# Dead-man's switch for outbound mail delivery, and the sibling of
# netdata-deadman in this directory.
#
# The probe *is* a mail message: a timer injects one into the local postfix
# addressed to a healthchecks.io check's email-ping address. Registering the
# ping requires the whole path to work -- postfix running, the chrooted
# resolver answering, an MX/A lookup for hc-ping.com, outbound :25 not
# blocked, and the queue actually moving. Break any link and the pings stop
# and healthchecks.io alerts to the same ntfy topic netdata uses.
#
# Why a round-trip probe instead of a queue-depth alert: this host carries a
# permanent backlog of undeliverable spam and backscatter that ages out on
# its own, so depth and even deferral *count* are uninformative -- any
# threshold is wrong by next month. A delivery that either happens or does
# not needs no threshold.
#
# This is deliberately a separate healthchecks.io check from netdata-deadman,
# not a second condition inside it, because the pair is diagnostic: both
# checks down means the host is gone; only this one down means the host is up
# and the mail path specifically is broken. Folding them together would throw
# that away.
#
# It notably covers the 2026-08-27 outage, where postfix's chrooted
# resolv.conf snapshot was captured empty during an unattended-upgrade and
# every remote delivery deferred for 23 hours (see
# postfix/files/chroot-resolv.conf). The probe would have deferred with the
# same DNS error and stopped pinging.
#
# HC_MAIL_ADDRESS is the check's email-ping address, <uuid>@hc-ping.com. The
# UUID is a forgeable-ping secret (anyone holding it can suppress the alert
# by pinging), so it lives out-of-band in /etc/default/mail-deadman rather
# than in the repo. If it's missing we exit non-zero on purpose: the failed
# unit trips netdata's systemdunits alert, so a misconfigured probe is itself
# noticed rather than silently never running.
#
# Note the UUID does land in /var/log/mail.log as the recipient of every
# probe, and in its rotated archives. That is accepted rather than solved:
# the log is 0640 root:adm on the same host that stores the secret, and the
# worst a leaked ping URL buys an attacker is the ability to keep this one
# alert quiet.

set -eu

if [ -r /etc/default/mail-deadman ]; then
    . /etc/default/mail-deadman
fi

: "${HC_MAIL_ADDRESS:?unset; put HC_MAIL_ADDRESS=<uuid>@hc-ping.com in /etc/default/mail-deadman}"

# Envelope sender, not just a From: header. Three reasons it is a real
# address in a local domain: a bounce comes back through aliases_regexp's
# catch-all to egnor@ofb.net, where a rejection is visible instead of lost;
# opendkim signs it; and eacs.io is in postsrsd's SRS_EXCLUDE_DOMAINS, so the
# envelope-from is left alone rather than SRS-rewritten.
: "${MAIL_DEADMAN_FROM:=mail-deadman@eacs.io}"

# sendmail(1) injects into the maildrop queue and succeeds even when postfix
# itself is stopped, so a zero exit here does NOT mean the mail was
# delivered -- that is the point. Only actual arrival registers the ping.
# A non-zero exit means we could not even inject, which is worth failing the
# unit for so systemdunits catches it.
printf 'From: %s\nTo: %s\nSubject: mail-deadman probe\n\nOutbound mail probe from egnor-2020. Source: dotfiles/deadman/files/mail-deadman\n' \
    "$MAIL_DEADMAN_FROM" "$HC_MAIL_ADDRESS" \
    | /usr/sbin/sendmail -f "$MAIL_DEADMAN_FROM" -- "$HC_MAIL_ADDRESS"
